#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Check only the changed lines of a git repository against language norms.

The scope (working tree, index, merge-base or tracked files) decides which
files and lines count as changed. Each language backend lints those files and
only the violations located on changed lines are reported.

Python: ruff with every rule and explicit complexity limits, ruff format,
a docstring on every definition and mypy --strict (LINT_STRICT_MYPY, else
the repo's .venv mypy, else uvx). JavaScript/TypeScript: the package's
eslint with typescript-eslint strict, explicit return types and complexity
limits, prettier, and tsc --noEmit per tsconfig (LINT_STRICT_TSC, else the
package's tsc). C/C++: clang-tidy with cognitive-complexity and
function-size limits, clang-format and, in Zephyr, checkpatch. A tool that
cannot run is listed under "Not checked". Every language also gets the
built-in magic-number rule: a magic number in a comparison (MAG001), a
value repeated on three lines (MAG002) and a number in a delay (MAG003).
The built-in habit rules (HAB<n>) report review habits that can be checked
mechanically, such as a StrEnum value that repeats its name.
"""

